<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Game configVersion="1">
  <!--
    Game configuration for the Microsoft GDK. This file must sit next to the
    executable at runtime, and is committed at the project root so the GDK
    initializes when you run the project directly from the editor.

    Identity, TitleId and MSAAppId together identify the title to Xbox
    services. They are filled in here with a working configuration so that
    sign-in and achievements (ids 1 and 2) function on a fresh clone.
    Replace all three to point the sample at your own title — see
    docs/configuration.md for the full checklist.

    The PlayFab title id is a different identifier for a different service and
    lives in project.godot under [playfab] runtime/title_id.

    AdvancedUserModel is false: the simplified user model, which is what the
    platform recommends for most titles. The advanced model exists for the
    minority that let the signed-in user change mid-session without a restart.
    NetRumble is not one of those — a user change closes the game (see
    XR-115) — so it has nothing to gain from the advanced model and takes the
    simpler contract instead.

    The simplified model requires valid TitleId and MSAAppId elements. Omit
    either and the process is killed with 0x89240102 at GDExtension load, with
    no other diagnostic; the flag is not the cause even though it looks like
    it.

    The model also has one consequence in code. Under the simplified model the
    platform supplies the launching user and rejects interactive adds with
    E_INVALIDARG, so sign-in has to come from the silent
    add_default_user_async(). IdentityService still calls
    add_user_with_ui_async() as a fallback; that call only does anything under
    the advanced model, and here it fails through to the "sign in to the Xbox
    app, then try again" message.

    Executable Name must match the exported .exe: the GDK export platform reads
    it from this file and renames Godot's output accordingly.

    One config, both targets. ExecutableList accepts unlimited Executable
    children, each declaring its own TargetDeviceFamily, so PC and console are
    described here instead of being switched by hand before every console
    export. The two entries use different executable names, and each pins its
    own Id: Ids must be unique across the list, and a duplicate fails config
    validation at package time.

    ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, and so is the wording of this comment. The
    "XBOX on PC" export platform derives the staged .exe name by regex-searching
    the raw file text for the first Executable element's Name attribute
    (addons/godot_gdk/editor/gdk_export_platform.gd,
    _read_exe_name_from_project_config). That search is not XML-aware: it scans
    comments too, so never spell an Executable start-tag with a Name attribute
    inside this comment block, or the export will name the binary after the
    comment. For the same reason the PC entry must stay first, or a PC export
    emits a console-named .exe. The console platform takes its name from the
    preset's export_path instead, which export_presets.cfg pins to
    NetRumbleConsole.exe to match.

    Keep this file free of trailing whitespace after the closing Game tag.
    Godot's XMLParser re-emits the root closing tag when anything follows it,
    which makes the GDK export plugin's parser pop past the bottom of its
    element stack and abort the export with
    "Index p_index = -1 is out of bounds (size() = 0)".
  -->

  <Identity Name="41336MicrosoftATG.NetRumble2"
            Publisher="CN=A4954634-DF4B-47C7-AB70-D3215D246AF1"
            Version="1.0.1.0" />

  <ExecutableList>
    <Executable Name="NetRumblePC.exe"
                Id="GamePC"
                TargetDeviceFamily="PC" />
    <Executable Name="NetRumbleConsole.exe"
                Id="GameConsole"
                TargetDeviceFamily="Scarlett" />
  </ExecutableList>
  <StoreId>9PN64QJC8BKL</StoreId>

  <TitleId>76B1590E</TitleId>
  <MSAAppId>0000000044264AE3</MSAAppId>

  <ShellVisuals
    DefaultDisplayName="NetRumble2"
    PublisherDisplayName="Xbox Advanced Technology Group"
    StoreLogo="storelogos\StoreLogo.png"
    Square150x150Logo="storelogos\Logo.png"
    Square44x44Logo="storelogos\SmallLogo.png"
    Square480x480Logo="storelogos\LargeLogo.png"
    Description="A Godot multiplayer sample demonstrating Microsoft GDK and PlayFab integration."
    ForegroundText="dark"
    BackgroundColor="#000000"
    SplashScreenImage="storelogos\SplashScreen.png" />

  <!-- Party uses protocol activation for multiplayer invites. DesktopRegistration
       is a PC-only element, so it names the PC executable. -->
  <DesktopRegistration>
    <MultiplayerProtocol Executable="NetRumblePC.exe">true</MultiplayerProtocol>
  </DesktopRegistration>

  <AdvancedUserModel>false</AdvancedUserModel>

</Game>